# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_vmo_create(uint64_t size, uint32_t options, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_vmo_create_physical(vx_handle resource, uint64_t pa,
                                 uint64_t size, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_vmo_create_pager(vx_handle pager, uint32_t key, uint64_t size,
                              vx_handle *out);

vx_status vx_vmo_clone(vx_handle vmo, uint64_t offset, uint64_t size,
                       vx_handle *out);

vx_status vx_vmo_rw(vx_handle vmo, enum vx_vmo_op op, uint64_t offset,
                    void *buf, uint64_t size);

vx_status vx_vmo_resize(vx_handle vmo, uint64_t size);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A VMO, a virtual memory object, is a run of pages named by a handle (see the kernel's design, 01 §5).
It is what a task maps with as_map(2), what a channel passes when it passes memory, and what a device driver maps to reach its registers.
A VMO's size is a whole number of 4096-byte pages and at most 256 MiB; a size that is not a multiple of a page is rounded up.
It lives until its last handle and its last mapping are gone.

There are three kinds.
An anonymous VMO's pages are all allocated and zeroed when it is made: memory is committed, not overcommitted, so a task learns it has run out from a failed call and never from a fault later.
A physical VMO is device memory, a fixed range of physical addresses that is not RAM.
A pager-backed VMO starts with no pages, and a trusted task supplies them as they are touched; pager(2) describes it.

`vx_vmo_create` makes an anonymous VMO of <size> bytes and puts a handle to it in <out>.
<options> is 0, or `VX_VMO_RESIZABLE` for one `vx_vmo_resize` may change (below): the other two kinds have calls of their own, below, which make the same system call with `VX_VMO_PHYSICAL` or `VX_VMO_PAGER`.
The handle has every right but `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`, `VX_RIGHT_EXEC` among them, since loaders and JITs map their own code; that a mapping is never both writable and executable is decided per mapping by as_map(2), not per VMO.

`vx_vmo_create_physical` makes a VMO over the device memory from <pa> to <pa> + <size>.
<resource> must be a Resource handle with `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`: drivers are given VMOs made this way, not the Resource.
<pa> and <size> must be page-aligned, and the range may not overlap RAM; the kernel refuses every physical VMO if the firmware's memory map left it unsure where RAM is.
The handle has `VX_RIGHT_READ`, `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, `VX_RIGHT_MAP`, `VX_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`, `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` and `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`: device memory is mapped, never executed.
Making a VMO over the range the kernel's console writes to hands the console over to the caller, so the kernel stops writing there, unless the kernel's command line holds `vx.kconsole`.

`vx_vmo_create_pager` makes a pager-backed VMO of <size> bytes whose pages <pager> supplies, none yet.
<pager> must be a Pager handle with `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, and <key>, a 32-bit number of the pager's choosing, names the VMO in the page requests the pager receives.
The handle has every right but `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`.

`vx_vmo_clone` makes a new anonymous VMO holding a copy of bytes <offset> to <offset> + <size> of <vmo> and puts a handle to it in <out>.
The copy is made at once and charged in full, as a clone is in 01 §5.
<vmo> needs `VX_RIGHT_READ`; <offset> and <size> must be page-aligned, <size> not zero, and the range within the VMO.
The new handle has the rights `vx_vmo_create` gives.
A physical or pager-backed VMO cannot be cloned.

`vx_vmo_rw` copies <size> bytes between offset <offset> of <vmo> and <buf> in the caller's memory: into <buf> when <op> is `VX_VMO_READ`, which needs `VX_RIGHT_READ`, and from it when <op> is `VX_VMO_WRITE`, which needs `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
The range may cross pages and need not be aligned, but must lie within the VMO.
It takes no page of a pager-backed VMO that its pager has not supplied, and asks for none: it copies up to the first such page and returns `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`.
A write to a pager-backed VMO marks the pages it writes dirty, as a store through a mapping would.
A physical VMO is not read or written this way; it is mapped.

`vx_vmo_resize` makes the system call `vmo_op` with `VX_VMO_RESIZE`: it asks for <vmo>'s size to become <size>, and needs `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
Only an anonymous VMO made with the option `VX_VMO_RESIZABLE` is resized (ADR-0042): pages added are zero; pages past a smaller size leave every mapping and are freed, so a touch there faults, and a mapping keeps its place.
Its pages are read under its lock, as a pager's are, so `vx_vmo_rw` on it goes through a small buffer; a VMO that will not be resized is better made without the option.
Any other anonymous VMO, and a physical one, is not resized.
A pager-backed one is its pager's to resize, with `pager_op` (see pager(2)), since everyone who maps it shares its size: `vmo_op` on one is refused.
`VX_VMO_RESIZE` is the only operation `vmo_op` has.

## Reserved calls

Three calls in `abi/vx/syscalls.def` are reserved, and have no vx-rt function; made directly, as `vx_syscall(VX_SYS_vmo_seal, ...)` and so on, each returns `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`:

: `vmo_seal`
  Will make a VMO read-only for all who hold it, so its bytes cannot change under a reader that checks them (01 §4.3, §6.6).
: `vmo_lease`
  Will make a revocable handle derived from a VMO's, to share memory that can be taken back (01 §3).
: `vmo_revoke`
  Will revoke a lease: the memory is unmapped from every task that maps it through the lease, and its DMA mappings are torn down.

# EXAMPLES

A write and a read across a page boundary, and a read past the end refused, from the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`):

```c
vx_handle vmo;
char in[8] = "abcdefg", out[8] = {};
CHECK(vx_vmo_create(8192, 0, &vmo) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_vmo_rw(vmo, VX_VMO_WRITE, 4092, in, 8) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_vmo_rw(vmo, VX_VMO_READ, 4092, out, 8) == VX_OK);
CHECK(out[0] == 'a' && out[6] == 'g');
CHECK(vx_vmo_rw(vmo, VX_VMO_READ, 8190, out, 8) == VX_ERR_RANGE);
vx_handle_close(vmo);
```

