# DIAGNOSTICS

`vx_vmo_create`, `vx_vmo_create_physical` and `vx_vmo_create_pager` return:

: `VX_OK`
  The VMO is made, and its handle is in <out>.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <options> holds a bit that is not `VX_VMO_PHYSICAL`, `VX_VMO_PAGER` or `VX_VMO_RESIZABLE`, or more than one of them; <key> does not fit 32 bits; or <out> is not writable memory of the caller's.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  <size> is 0 or more than 256 MiB; for a physical VMO, <pa> or <size> is not page-aligned, or the range wraps.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <resource> or <pager> is not a handle to a Resource or a Pager.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <resource> lacks `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` or <pager> lacks `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`; or the physical range overlaps RAM, or the kernel does not know all of RAM.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  There is no memory for the pages, the page list, or a handle.

`vx_vmo_clone` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  The copy is made, and its handle is in <out>.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  The options, which the vx-rt function always passes as 0, are not 0; or <out> is not writable.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <vmo> is not a handle to a VMO.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <vmo> lacks `VX_RIGHT_READ`.
: `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`
  <vmo> is physical or pager-backed.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  <size> is 0, <offset> or <size> is not page-aligned, or the range does not lie within the VMO.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  There is no memory for the copy or its handle.

`vx_vmo_rw` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  Every byte was copied.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <op> is neither `VX_VMO_READ` nor `VX_VMO_WRITE`, or <buf> is not memory of the caller's that the copy may use; a copy that faults partway leaves the bytes before the fault copied.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <vmo> is not a handle to a VMO.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <vmo> lacks the right <op> needs.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  The range does not lie within the VMO.
: `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`
  <vmo> is physical.
: `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`
  <vmo> is pager-backed and a page of the range has not been supplied; the bytes before it were copied.

`vx_vmo_resize` returns:

: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  The operation is not `VX_VMO_RESIZE`.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <vmo> is not a handle to a VMO.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <vmo> lacks `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, or is pager-backed.
: `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`
  <vmo> was not made `VX_VMO_RESIZABLE`, or is physical.

`vmo_seal`, `vmo_lease` and `vmo_revoke` return `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`.

# BUGS

The design's other operations (commit, decommit, cache cleaning and invalidation, lock, hint, purgeable, priority; 01 §3, §5) are not there, and neither are seals, leases or the `NODUMP` mark.

A clone is a full copy made at once, not pages shared until written; the design's copy-on-write is an optimization for later behind the same call.

The design's contiguous VMOs, NUMA placement, cache policy per VMO and carve-outs (`VX_VMO_CARVEOUT`) are not there: a physical VMO is always mapped as device memory.

VMOs are not yet charged to a memory budget: commit is enforced only by the allocator running out.

A VMO is limited to 256 MiB, the most one block of its page list can name.

`vx_vmo_rw` on a pager-backed VMO copies through a small buffer, 256 bytes at a time, to hold the VMO's lock while it touches a page; it is slow for large copies.
